.Dd June 9, 2024
.Dt SOXFORMAT 7
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm soxformat
.Nd audio formats supported by SoX
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Pp
Format types that SoX can determine by a filename extension
are listed under that extension.
Format types that are optionally built into SoX are marked
.Dq optional .
Format types that can be handled by sndfile via
.Dq Fl t Ic sndfile
are listed under
.Dq sndfile .
This might be useful if you have a file that doesn't work
with SoX's default format readers and writers,
but there is an external reader or writer for that format.
.Pp
.Bl -tag -width xx
.It raw, f32, f64, s8, s16, s24, s32, u8, u16, u24, u32, ul, al, lu, la
Raw (headerless) audio files.
The sample rate and the data encoding
must be given using command-line options;
for the other listed types,
the sample rate defaults to 8kHz (but may be overridden),
and the data encoding is defined by the given suffix.
Thus f32 and f64 indicate files encoded as
32 and 64-bit floating point PCM, respectively;
s8, s16, s24, and s32 indicate 8, 16, 24,
and 32-bit signed integer PCM, respectively;
u8, u16, u24, and u32 indicate 8, 16, 24,
and 32-bit unsigned integer PCM, respectively;
ul indicates `\(*m-law' (8-bit), al indicates `A-law' (8-bit), and
lu and la are inverse bit order `\(*m-law' and inverse bit order `A-law'.
The number of channels defaults to 1, but may be overridden.
See
.Dq ima
and
.Dq vox
for raw ADPCM formats, and
.Dq cdda
for raw CD digital audio.
.It 8svx
Amiga 8SVX musical instrument description format.
.It aiff, aif
AIFF files as used on old Apple Macs, Apple IIc/IIgs and SGI.
SoX's AIFF support does not include multiple audio chunks,
or the 8SVX musical instrument description format.
AIFF files are multimedia archives and
can have multiple audio and picture chunks;
you may need a separate tool to work with them.
AIFF has been superseded by CAF.
.It aiffc, aifce
AIFF-C is a format based on AIFF
that was created to allow handling compressed audio.
It can also handle little endian uncompressed linear data
that is often referred to as
.Dq sowt
encoding.
This encoding has also become the defacto format produced
by modern Macs as well as iTunes on any platform.
AIFF-C files produced by other applications
typically have the file extension
.Dq aif
and require looking at its header to detect the true format.
The
.Dq sowt
encoding is the only encoding that SoX can handle with this format.
.SP
AIFF-C is defined in DAVIC 1.4 Part 9 Annex B.
This format is referred from ARIB STD-B24,
which is specified for Japanese data broadcasting.
No private chunks are supported.
.It alsa (optional)
Advanced Linux Sound Architecture device driver;
supports both playing and recording audio.
ALSA is only used in Linux-based operating systems,
though these might support OSS (see below) as well.
.It amb
Ambisonic B-Format: a specialisation of WAV
with between 3 and 16 channels of audio
for use with an Ambisonic decoder.
It is up to the user to get the channels together
in the right order and at the correct amplitude.
.It anb (optional)
Adaptive Multi Rate Narrow Band speech codec;
a lossy format used in 3rd generation mobile telephony
and defined in 3GPP TS 26.071 et al.
AMR-NB audio has a fixed sampling rate of 8 kHz
and supports encoding to the following bit-rates,
selected by the
.Fl C
option of
.Xr sox 1 :
.Pp
.Bl -tag -compact
.It 0 = 4.75 kbit/s
.It 1 = 5.15 kbit/s
.It 2 = 5.9 kbit/s
.It 3 = 6.7 kbit/s
.It 4 = 7.4 kbit/s
.It 5 = 7.95 kbit/s
.It 6 = 10.2 kbit/s
.It 7 = 12.2 kbit/s
.El
.It amr, awb (optional)
Adaptive Multi Rate Wide Band speech codec;
a lossy format used in 3rd generation mobile telephony
and defined in 3GPP TS 26.171 et al.
AMR-WB audio has a fixed sampling rate of 16 kHz
and supports encoding to the following bit-rates,
as selected by the
.Fl C
option.
.Pp
.Bl -tag -compact
.It 0 = 6.6 kbit/s
.It 1 = 8.85 kbit/s
.It 2 = 12.65 kbit/s
.It 3 = 14.25 kbit/s
.It 4 = 15.85 kbit/s
.It 5 = 18.25 kbit/s
.It 6 = 19.85 kbit/s
.It 7 = 23.05 kbit/s
.It 8 = 23.85 kbit/s
.El
.It ao (optional)
Xiph.org's Audio Output device driver; works only for playing.
It supports a wide range of devices and sound systems,
see its documentation for the full range.
.It au, snd
Sun Microsystems AU files.
There are many types of AU files;
DEC has its own with a different magic number
and little-endian byte order.
To write a DEC file, use the
.Fl L
option for the output file.
.SP
Some .au files are known to have invalid AU headers;
these are probably original Sun \(*m-law 8000 Hz files
and can be dealt with using the
.Dq ul
format (see below).
.It avr
Audio Visual Research format;
used by a number of commercial packages
on the Mac.
.It caf (optional)
Apple's Core Audio File format.
.IT cdda, cdr
`Red Book' Compact Disc Digital Audio (raw audio).  CDDA has two audio
channels formatted as 16-bit signed integers (big endian)at a sample
rate of 44\*d1\ kHz.  The number of (stereo) samples in each CDDA
track is always a multiple of 588.
.It coreaudio (optional)
MacOS CoreAudio device driver; supports both playing and recording.
If a filename is not specific or if the name is "default" then
the default audio device is selected.  Any other name will be used
to select a specific device.  The valid names can be seen in the
System Preferences->Sound menu and then under the Output and Input tabs.
Examples:
.EX
	sox infile \-t coreaudio
	sox infile \-t coreaudio default
	sox infile \-t coreaudio "Internal Speakers"
.EE
.\" FIXME Say this generally for output drivers.
.\" See also
.\".BR play (1),
.\".BR rec (1),
.\"and
.\".BR sox (1)
.\".BR \-d .
.It cvs, cvsd
Continuously Variable Slope Delta modulation.
A headerless format used to compress speech audio
for applications such as voice mail.
This format is sometimes used with bit-reversed samples; the
.Fl -X
format option can be used to set the bit-order.
.It cvu
Continuously Variable Slope Delta modulation (unfiltered).
This is an alternative handler for CVSD that is unfiltered but can
be used with any bit-rate.  E.g.
.EX
	sox infile outfile.cvu rate 28k
	play \-r 28k outfile.cvu sinc \-3.4k
.EE
.It dat
Text Data files.
These files contain a textual representation of the sample data.
There is one line at the beginning that contains the sample rate,
and one line that contains the number of channels.
Subsequent lines contain two or more numeric data intems:
the time since the beginning of the first sample
and the sample value for each channel.
Values are normalized to [-1,1].
This file format can be used to create data files
for external programs such as FFT analysers or graph routines.
.It dvms, vms
Used in Germany to compress speech audio for voice mail.
A self-describing variant of the cvsd format.
.It fap
See paf.
.It flac (optional)
Free Lossless Audio CODEC compressed audio.
FLAC is an open, patent-free CODEC designed for compressing music.
SoX can read native FLAC files (.flac) but not Ogg FLAC files (.ogg);
see
.Dq ogg
below for support of Ogg Vorbis.
SoX can write native FLAC files according to a given or default compression level:
8 is the default compression level and gives the best (but slowest) compression;
0 gives the least (but fastest) compression.
The compression level is selected using the
.Fl C
option.
.It gsrt
Grandstream ring-tone files.
Whilst this file format can contain A-Law, \(*m-law,
GSM, G.722, G.723, G.726, G.728, or iLBC encoded audio,
SoX only supports reading and writing A-Law and \(*m-law.
.It gsm (optional)
GSM 06.10 Lossy Speech Compression.
A lossy format for compressing speech which is used
in the Global Standard for Mobile telecommunications (GSM).
It's good for its purpose, shrinking audio data size,
but it will introduce lots of noise when a given audio signal
is encoded and decoded multiple times.
This format is used by some voice mail applications.
.B hcom
Macintosh HCOM files.
These are Mac FSSD files with Huffman compression.
.It htk
Single channel 16-bit PCM format used by HTK,
a toolkit for building Hidden Markov Model speech processing tools.
.It ima
A headerless file of IMA ADPCM audio data.
IMA ADPCM claims 16-bit precision packed into only 4 bits,
but in fact sounds no better than
.Sq vox .
.It lpc (optional)
LPC-10 is a compression scheme for speech.
There is no associated file format, so SoX's implementation is headerless.
.It mat, mat4, mat5 (optional)
Matlab 4.2/5.0 format; mat is the same as mat4.
.It m3u
A playlist format; contains a list of audio files.
SoX can read, but not write this file format.
.It maud
An IFF-conforming audio file type,
registered by MS MacroSystem Computer GmbH,
published along with the `Toccata' sound-card on the Amiga.
Allows 8bit linear, 16bit linear, A-Law, \(*m-law in mono and stereo.
.It mp3, mp2 (optional)
MP3 compressed audio;
MP3 (MPEG Layer 3) is a part of the MPEG standards for audio and video compression.
When reading MP3 files, up to 28 bits of precision is stored
although only 16 bits is reported to user.
This is to allow the default behavior of writing 16 bit output files.
A user can specify a higher precision for the output file
to prevent lossing this extra information.
MP3 output files will use up to 24 bits of precision while encoding.
MP3 compression parameters can be selected using the
.FL C
option as follows.
The primary parameter to the LAME encoder is the bit rate.
If the argument to
.Fl C
is a positive integer, it is taken as the bitrate in kbps.
The second most important parameter is probably
.Dq quality
(means performance really), which allows balancing encoding speed vs. quality.
In LAME, 0 specifies highest quality but is very slow,
while 9 selects poor quality, but is fast.
The value of 5 (no more, no less) is the default
and 2 is recommended as a good trade-off for high quality encoding.
The
.Fl C
argument is a float and the fractional part is used to select quality,
so 128.2 selects 128 kbps encoding with a quality of 2.
There is one problem with this approach. We need 128 to specify
128 kbps encoding with default quality, so 0 means use default.
Instead of 0, use .01 to specify the highest quality.
LAME uses bitrate to specify a constant bitrate,
but higher quality can be achieved using Variable Bit Rate (VBR).
VBR quality (meaning size really) is selected using a number from 0 to 9.
Use a value of 0 for high quality, larger files, and 9 for smaller files of lower quality.
The default is 4.
In order to squeeze the selection of VBR into the the
.FL C
float argument we use negative numbers to select VRR.
For example, -4.2 selects the default VBR encoding (size) with high quality (speed).
One special case is 0, which is a valid VBR encoding parameter but not a valid bitrate.
Compression value of 0 is always treated as a high quality vbr,
and as a result both -0.2 and 0.2 are treated as
highest quality VBR (size) and high quality (speed).
.It nist
See sph.
.It ogg, vorbis (optional)
Ogg Vorbis compressed audio; an open, patent-free CODEC
designed for music and streaming audio.
It is a lossy compression format that achieves
good compression rates with a minimum amount of quality loss.
SoX can decode all types of Ogg Vorbis files,
and can encode at different compression levels/qualities
given as a number from -1 (highest compression/lowest quality)
to 10 (lowest compression, highest quality).
By default the encoding quality level is 3
(which gives an encoded rate of approx. 112 kbps);
this can be changed using the
.Fl C
option with a number from -1 to 10;
fractional numbers are also allowed.
.It opus (optional)
Xiph.org's Opus compressed audio; an open, lossy, low-latency codec
offering a wide range of compression rates. It uses the Ogg container.
SoX can only read Opus files, not write them.
.It oss (optional)
Open Sound System
.Pa /dev/dsp device driver; supports both playing and recording.
OSS support is used by FreeBSD and some other Unix operating systems.
.It paf, fap
Ensoniq PARIS file format (big and little-endian, respectively).
.It pls
A playlist format; contains a list of audio files.
SoX can read, but not write this file format.
See [2] for details of this format.
.SP
Note: SoX support for SHOUTcast PLS relies on
.BR wget (1)
and is only partially supported: it's necessary to
specify the audio type manually, e.g.
.EX
	play \-t mp3 \(dqhttp://a.server/pls?rn=265&file=filename.pls\(dq
.EE
and SoX does not know about alternative servers\*mhit Ctrl-C twice in
quick succession to quit.
.It prc
Psion Record. Used in Psion EPOC PDAs (Series 5, Revo and similar) for
System alarms and recordings made by the built-in Record application.
When writing, SoX defaults to A-law, which is recommended; if you must
use ADPCM, then use the \fB\-e ima-adpcm\fR switch. The sound quality is poor
because Psion Record seems to insist on frames of 800 samples or
fewer, so that the ADPCM CODEC has to be reset at every 800 frames,
which causes the sound to glitch every tenth of a second.
.It pulseaudio (optional)
PulseAudio driver; supports both playing and recording of audio.
PulseAudio is a cross platform networked sound server.
.It pvf
Portable Voice Format.
.It sd2
Sound Designer 2 format.
.It sds (optional)
MIDI Sample Dump Standard.
.It sf
IRCAM SDIF (Institut de Recherche et Coordination Acoustique/Musique
Sound Description Interchange Format).
Used by music software such as the CSound package,
and the MixView sound sample editor.
.It sln
Asterisk PBX `signed linear' 8khz, 16-bit signed integer, little-endian
raw format.
.It sph, nist
SPHERE (SPeech HEader Resources) is a file format defined by NIST
(National Institute of Standards and Technology) and is used with
speech audio.  SoX can read these files when they contain
\(*m-law and PCM data.  It will ignore any header information that
says the data is compressed using \fIshorten\fR compression and
will treat the data as either \(*m-law or PCM.  This will allow SoX
and the command line \fIshorten\fR program to be run together using
pipes to encompasses the data and then pass the result to SoX for processing.
.It smp
Turtle Beach SampleVision files.
SMP files are for use with the PC-DOS package SampleVision by Turtle Beach
Softworks.  This package is for communication to several MIDI samplers.  All
sample rates are supported by the package, although not all are supported by
the samplers themselves.  Currently loop points are ignored.
.It snd
See
.Dq au ,
.Dq sndr
and
.Dq sndt .
.Dq sndfile (optional)
This is a pseudo-type that forces libsndfile to be used. For writing files, the
actual file type is then taken from the output file name; for reading
them, it is deduced from the file.
.It sndio (optional)
OpenBSD audio device driver; supports both playing and recording audio.
.It sndr
Sounder files.
An MS-DOS/Windows format from the early '90s.
Sounder files usually have the extension `.SND'.
.It sndt
SoundTool files.
An MS-DOS/Windows format from the early '90s.
SoundTool files usually have the extension `.SND'.
.It .sox
SoX's native uncompressed PCM format, intended for storing (or piping)
audio at intermediate processing points (i.e. between SoX invocations).
It has much in common with the popular WAV, AIFF, and AU uncompressed PCM
formats, but has the following specific characteristics: the PCM samples
are always stored as 32 bit signed integers, the samples are stored (by
default) as `native endian', and the number of samples in the file is
recorded as a 64-bit integer.  Comments are also supported.
See `Special Filenames' in
.Xr sox 1
for examples of using the
sox format with pipes.
.It sunaudio
\fBsunau\fR (optional)
Sun Microsystems
.Pa /dev/audio
device driver; supports both playing and recording.
.It txw
Yamaha TX-16W sampler.
A file format from a Yamaha sampling keyboard which wrote IBM-PC
format 3\*d5\(dq floppies.  Handles reading of files which do not have
the sample rate field set to one of the expected by looking at some
other bytes in the attack/loop length fields, and defaulting to
33\ kHz if the sample rate is still unknown.
.It vms
See dvms.
.It voc
Sound Blaster VOC files.
VOC files are multi-part and contain silence parts, looping, and
different sample rates for different chunks.
On input, the silence parts are filled out, loops are rejected,
and sample data with a new sample rate is rejected.
Silence with a different sample rate is generated appropriately.
On output, silence is not detected, nor are impossible sample rates.
SoX supports reading (but not writing) VOC files with multiple
blocks, and files containing \(*m-law, A-law, and 2/3/4-bit ADPCM samples.
.It vorbis
See ogg.
.It vox
A headerless file of Dialogic/OKI ADPCM audio data commonly comes with the
extension .vox.  This ADPCM data has 12-bit precision packed into only 4-bits.
.SP
Note: some early Dialogic hardware does not always reset the ADPCM
encoder at the start of each vox file.  This can result in clipping
and/or DC offset problems when it comes to decoding the audio.  Whilst
little can be done about the clipping, a DC offset can be removed by
passing the decoded audio through a high-pass filter, e.g.:
.EX
	sox input.vox output.wav highpass 10
.EE
.It w64
Sonic Foundry's 64-bit RIFF/WAV format.
.It wav
Microsoft .WAV RIFF files.
This is the native audio file format of Windows,
and widely used for uncompressed audio.
.SP
Normally \fB.wav\fR files have all formatting information
in their headers, and so do not need any format options
specified for an input file.  If any are, they will
override the file header, and you will be warned to this effect.
You had better know what you are doing! Output format
options will cause a format conversion, and the \fB.wav\fR
will written appropriately.
.SP
SoX can read and write linear PCM, floating point, \(*m-law, A-law, MS ADPCM, and IMA (or DVI) ADPCM encoded samples.
WAV files can also contain audio encoded in many other ways (not currently
supported with SoX) e.g. MP3; in some cases such a file can still be
read by SoX by overriding the file type, e.g.
.EX
   play \-t mp3 mp3\-encoded.wav
.EE
Big endian versions of RIFF files, called RIFX, are also supported.
To write a RIFX file, use the
.B \-B
option with the output file options.
.It waveaudio (optional)
MS-Windows native audio device driver.
.EX
	sox infile \-t waveaudio
	sox infile \-t waveaudio default
	sox infile \-t waveaudio 1
	sox infile \-t waveaudio "High Definition Audio Device ("
.EE
If the device name is omitted, \fB-1\fR, or \fBdefault\fR, then you
get the `Microsoft Wave Mapper' device.  Wave Mapper means `use the
system default audio devices'.  You can control what `default' means
via the OS Control Panel.
.SP
If the device name given is some other number, you get that audio
device by index; so recording with device name \fB0\fR would get the
first input device (perhaps the microphone), \fB1\fR would get the
second (perhaps line in), etc.  Playback using \fB0\fR will get the
first output device (usually the only audio device).
.SP
If the device name given is something other than a number, SoX tries
to match it (maximum 31 characters) against the names of the available
devices.
.It wavpcm
A non-standard, but widely used, variant of
.BR .wav .
Some applications cannot read a standard WAV file header for PCM-encoded
data with sample-size greater than 16-bits or with more than two
channels, but can read a non-standard
WAV header.  It is likely that such applications will eventually be
updated to support the standard header, but in the mean time, this SoX
format can be used to create files with the non-standard header that
should work with these applications.  (Note that SoX will automatically
detect and read WAV files with the non-standard header.)
.SP
The most common use of this file-type is likely to be along the following
lines:
.EX
	sox infile.any \-t wavpcm \-e signed-integer outfile.wav
.EE
.It wv (optional)
WavPack lossless audio compression.  Note that, when converting
.B .wav
to this format and back again,
the RIFF header is not necessarily preserved losslessly (though the audio is).
.It wve
Psion 8-bit A-law, used on Psion SIBO PDAs (Series 3 and similar).
.\" FIXME are the following really supported by sndfile?
.\"raw
.\"aiff
.\"au
.\"flac
.\"gsm
.\"ima
.\"ircam
.\"nist
.\"ogg
.\"opus
.\"sf
.\"snd
.\"sph
.\"voc
.\"vorbis
.\"vox
.\"wav
.\"wve
.It xa
Maxis XA files.
These are 16-bit ADPCM audio files used by Maxis games.  Writing .xa files is
currently not supported, although adding write support should not be very
difficult.
.It xi
Fasttracker 2 Extended Instrument format.
.El
